I've written the session plan: two task files plus the issue responses. The plan commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused. The plan command ends in || true, which suggests the harness expects that; the files are on disk.

Task 1 (product, `src/tools.rs`): kill the whole process group when a bash command times out or is cancelled. Today yoyo's own bash tool kills only the bash process. The assessment showed a pipeline stage like sleep … | cat keeps running after its parent bash is killed, so a timed-out build or dev server is left behind. The steps are:
1. Read the same fix in yoagent 0.25.2's BashTool first.
2. Write a test that fails now: after a timeout, the unique sleep must be gone.
3. Fix it using process_group(0) plus a group kill, with the existing libc dependency.
4. Confirm the Ctrl+C path still reaches the tool's cancel branch, since the terminal's Ctrl+C will no longer reach the child directly.
5. Pin two near-misses: normal completion and non-zero exit codes behave as before.
6. Break the fix on purpose to check the test catches it, then restore it.

This is in the tools subsystem, which follows the warning that format has had too many recent changes.

Task 2 (product, part of #982): two shell commands that exit 0 on failure.
• `yoyo map <missing path>` reports "no supported source files", which makes a typo look like a real, empty project. It will exit 1 with the error on stderr. A real directory that is empty stays at exit 0, pinned by a test.
• yoyo def and yoyo outline with no argument will exit 2.
• The task reproduces the exact commands before and after, and rewrites #982's body to list what is actually left.

Not planned: the yoagent 0.24.2 → 0.25.2 upgrade. A grep showed it touches far more than the 3-file limit per task:
• the enable_bundled() call for prices, placed where tests run too;
• 5 `Extension → Custom` renames in 3 files;
• 22 version markers across about 10 files, which tests/doc_version_claims.rs fails on until each paragraph is re-read;
• a check of how Ctrl+C reaches the sub-agent's bash.

A compile break means it can't be split across sessions, so rather than leave it as a line in the assessment I filed #1007 with that list, for a session that plans the upgrade as its only task. Until it lands, the main agent's bash will kill the whole group on timeout while the sub-agent's bash still kills only bash.

No release this session; v0.2.0 was 4 days ago. No community issues need a reply.
